WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:24.870
Hello.

9
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:26.350
Hey there, everybody.

10
00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:28.870
We have such a fun show for you today.

11
00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:29.790
We do.

12
00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:34.430
We got to interview Phyllis Smith all about her time on The Office.

13
00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:40.950
You know, a while back when we rewatched Phyllis's wedding, well, Phyllis sent in some great audio clips about that episode.

14
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:46.610
But we were so excited to finally have her on the podcast as a guest to do a full interview.

15
00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.030
She told us how she got her role in The Office.

16
00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:52.030
It's such a fantastic story.

17
00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:56.070
I think it's maybe the best story of how someone got their job on The Office.

18
00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.130
Yeah, the best origin story.

19
00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:57.390
Yeah.

20
00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:03.010
We also asked her all of our usual questions, you know, and Angela made sure to ask her our call sheet questions,

21
00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:05.710
which are now one of my favorite things of our interviews.

22
00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.310
You just find out such interesting things about people.

23
00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:12.310
And, you know, one of the reasons I think I like it is because we know these people,

24
00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.510
and I feel like we've known them for so long.

25
00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:14.770
Right.

26
00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.150
And then I learned something new.

27
00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:16.710
Right.

28
00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:21.030
And we also get to talk to Phyllis about her new movie, Inside Out 2.

29
00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:22.370
It's in theaters now.

30
00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.550
My family loved the first Inside Out.

31
00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.170
We watched it so many times.

32
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:28.630
I can't wait to see this one.

33
00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:29.590
Oh, same.

34
00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.430
My daughter has already clocked it.

35
00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:32.250
We have plans.

36
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.910
We're going to see it with her cousins.

37
00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:34.550
Oh, so cute.

38
00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:36.690
Like, it's going to be a family event movie for us.

39
00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.470
Everybody knows the Inside Out movies, right?

40
00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:40.730
It's an animated movie.

41
00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:41.630
It's from Pixar.

42
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.270
So, in the first movie, there's a little girl.

43
00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.650
Her name is Riley, and she has to move to a new city.

44
00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:53.290
And in this process, we get to meet all the little emotions that are living in her head.

45
00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:55.330
And they are voiced by different actors.

46
00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:56.310
Like, there's joy.

47
00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:58.790
That's kind of the leading emotion in her head.

48
00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.590
Joy is played by Amy Poehler.

49
00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:02.750
But there's also all these other emotions.

50
00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:04.850
There's fear, anger, disguSt.

51
00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:05.670
There's sadness.

52
00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:10.050
And they kind of all come together to help Riley with this new transition.

53
00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:13.170
And Phyllis plays the voice of sadness.

54
00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.790
She's so amazing in this role.

55
00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.310
And she's back playing sadness for Inside Out 2.

56
00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:24.230
I guess Riley is now a teenager, and she's going through puberty, which brings up a lot of new emotions.

57
00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:28.850
And Phyllis talks to us about what is so meaningful to her about these movies.

58
00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.690
And it's just a really lovely interview.

59
00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:32.050
It really is.

60
00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:35.450
I thought that it went to some just really sweet places.

61
00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:39.870
And I think it's such a great representation of who Phyllis is as a person.

62
00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:41.350
I'm really proud of it, actually.

63
00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:45.330
I thought her recall of Office stuff was so good.

64
00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.490
She remembers so much stuff.

65
00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:47.870
I know.

66
00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:49.630
I just loved reminiscing with her.

67
00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:50.550
Me too.

68
00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:53.010
Well, you guys, why don't we take a break?

69
00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.390
And when we come back, it's our interview with Phyllis.

70
00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:06.570
We made it.

71
00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:07.490
Woo-hoo!

72
00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:11.130
I was going to start out being really sad for you.

73
00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.690
Oh, you were going to be sadness.

74
00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:17.030
Yes, I was just going to say hi, guys.

75
00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:18.910
How are you?

76
00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:23.950
I'm really happy to be here to talk to you all.

77
00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:26.610
So, it's a good day.

78
00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:28.910
Oh, my gosh.

79
00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:29.770
I love it.

80
00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:36.430
Phyllis, Inside Out was such a huge hit in our family, the first one.

81
00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:39.710
I'm so excited there's going to be a second one.

82
00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:43.690
And it just tickles me so much that your voice is in this movie.

83
00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.290
Oh, I'll tell you.

84
00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:48.110
I'm excited to see Inside Out 2 as well.

85
00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:51.870
Phyllis, we watched it so many times.

86
00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:55.510
It's one of the movies my kids have watched over and over.

87
00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:56.330
Same.

88
00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:57.870
It's just so fantastic.

89
00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:05.090
Are your kids drawn to certain emotions more than others or just in general?

90
00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:06.250
In general.

91
00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:07.390
In general.

92
00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:11.550
I think they like that there's this distinction between.

93
00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:16.970
I mean, that's what I like about it as a parent is that we get to see all the different emotions

94
00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:18.770
and then we get to see them work together.

95
00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:19.890
Right.

96
00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:20.750
Yeah.

97
00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:28.310
You know how I got this part was one of the producers on the show couldn't sleep one night

98
00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:34.510
and he threw in Bad Teacher, which was Gene and...

99
00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:35.590
Lee's movie.

100
00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:36.470
Yeah.

101
00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:38.530
That you did with Cameron Diaz.

102
00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:39.410
Yes.

103
00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:40.050
Yeah.

104
00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:47.510
And he couldn't sleep and he threw that DVD in and he immediately, when he heard my voice,

105
00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:50.470
he called Pete Docter and said, I found our sadness.

106
00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:51.790
Wow.

107
00:04:52.650 --> 00:05:00.110
And so, yeah, it's interesting how things just kind of go from one project to another.

108
00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:04.610
Had I not done Bad Teacher, I wouldn't have been sadness, you know?

109
00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:10.430
I had an acting teacher when I first was in Los Angeles who used to say, work begets work.

110
00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:11.570
Take the job.

111
00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:12.650
Absolutely.

112
00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:13.930
Yeah, absolutely.

113
00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:19.430
Because you never know how it's going to connect to the next part of your life, you know?

114
00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:28.850
So I was really, when I actually got the call for that movie for Inside Out 1, I was sitting

115
00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:32.930
here on our sofa and the phone rang and I didn't recognize the number and I answered it.

116
00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:36.270
It was the casting director, the Pixar casting director.

117
00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:41.010
And they said, they'd like for you to come to Pixar to meet him.

118
00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:41.810
And I went, okay.

119
00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:49.810
And so that was like in 2013, maybe.

120
00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:55.230
With Inside Out 1, I did about 10 different sessions in studios.

121
00:05:55.230 --> 00:06:00.750
All of them were in, most of them were at Disney on their studio and some up at Pixar.

122
00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:06.610
But unlike with Inside Out 2, because I'm not traveling, I did all of them here in St.

123
00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:06.870
Louis.

124
00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:10.710
They were kind enough to allow me to tape here in St.

125
00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:11.550
Louis, so.

126
00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:13.430
That's so fantastic.

127
00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:16.090
You got to make your big star demand.

128
00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:18.450
Right?

129
00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:24.310
You're like, you can have me back as sadness, but only if you come to my town this time.

130
00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:27.190
That's probably how you said it, right?

131
00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:28.930
Yeah, there you go.

132
00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:32.450
I forgot about those big star demands.

133
00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:34.750
Darn it, it's all too late now.

134
00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:39.510
Well, had you ever done voiceover work like that before, Phyllis?

135
00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:40.270
No.

136
00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:47.530
When I, my first session was at a Disney studio and I kept looking, this is so silly.

137
00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:51.290
There was the music stand and there was a pencil there.

138
00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:53.810
And I kept thinking, what do they want me to do with the pencil?

139
00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:56.430
Because I'd never done voiceover before.

140
00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:01.150
And the fact that they change it mid-sentence and you write it down.

141
00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:02.390
I didn't know any of that.

142
00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:07.310
And so I was so bothered by the pencil lying there going, what am I supposed to do with it?

143
00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:09.230
I know I'm supposed to do something with it.

144
00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:13.410
And then I found out later on in the session.

145
00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:21.990
But I was fortunate enough then when Amy was hired, Amy and I did about maybe six sessions together.

146
00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:32.870
And it was kind of an on-site learning experience for me because I could watch her and learn to do it just on-site.

147
00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:33.410
I did it.

148
00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:36.710
And so now it feels pretty comfortable and I like it.

149
00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:37.910
Nice.

150
00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:38.970
I like to do more.

151
00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:39.410
Yeah.

152
00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:43.970
Well, Phyllis, thank you so much for coming on Office Ladies today.

153
00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:47.410
You have no idea how excited we are to get to interview you.

154
00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:51.910
Yeah, I was, I was excited and also nervous for whatever reason.

155
00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:53.870
Oh gosh, Phyllis.

156
00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:55.230
Where'd you stop it?

157
00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:56.570
I was.

158
00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:58.450
It's just us gals.

159
00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:00.050
I know these gals.

160
00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:00.590
Yeah.

161
00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:03.190
I mean, you're the reason we have our job.

162
00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:06.010
That's right.

163
00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:07.590
That's true.

164
00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:13.430
You know, speaking of work begets work and how you never know how one thing might come from another,

165
00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:16.710
will you tell our listeners how you got your job on the office?

166
00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:17.910
Oh, sure.

167
00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:18.310
Yeah.

168
00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:21.090
I was the casting associate on the show.

169
00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:23.350
I'd been in casting about 19 years.

170
00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:27.170
I was working with Allison Jones, the casting director.

171
00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:43.530
And we were at the point in the casting process where we were pairing up the actors to see what their chemistry would be together and putting them on tape for the producers and also for the network.

172
00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:45.650
Eventually, it was going to go to the network.

173
00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:56.110
And I remember, and we were in this very odd building where the actors sat at one end and the auditions were at the other end.

174
00:08:56.110 --> 00:09:06.410
And I remember Kim Kloppis, the director, he was getting his coffee together and he turned around to me and he said, Phyllis, I want you to read the character of Pam today.

175
00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:08.270
I went, what?

176
00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:10.970
And he said, I want you to read the character of Pam.

177
00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:12.070
I went, okay.

178
00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:17.330
Because at this point, the associate wasn't really reading with the actors.

179
00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:22.750
Like I said, they were pairing the Pam and the Jims and the Dwights and everybody together.

180
00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:28.390
And I was a little taken back because I didn't want to step on like Allison Jones' toes.

181
00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:29.870
She was the boss.

182
00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:35.050
I didn't know if they had cleared it with her or not, but I went ahead and did it.

183
00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:41.830
And I was so nervous, again, because I wanted to do a good job, not for me, but for the actors.

184
00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:47.890
I wanted to make sure that I was right there when they needed me, timing-wise and such.

185
00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:58.190
And one of the first pair, I was Pam and it was with Krasinski and another person who was going to audition for Dwight.

186
00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:03.750
And then eventually I played Dwight with two other people.

187
00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:10.910
And so no one ever, Greg Daniels or no one ever said to me, Phyllis, do you want to be in the show?

188
00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:20.510
And I remember a fax came through to our office with an updated script and it had Phyllis in it.

189
00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.350
And Allison goes, is this Phyllis?

190
00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:24.570
And I never heard anything.

191
00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:27.050
You know, she goes, is this Phyllis, my Phyllis?

192
00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:29.050
But I never heard the answer.

193
00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:32.950
And then what happened was Wardrobe called.

194
00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:39.630
I had one line in the pilot, Wardrobe called and said, I understand you're playing the character of Phyllis.

195
00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:43.150
And I said, yes, I am.

196
00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:54.110
Because I knew from the union rules that if Wardrobe called you or if you were sent a script, that constitutes a work call.

197
00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:57.390
And so when Wardrobe called, I knew it was to be.

198
00:10:57.570 --> 00:10:58.730
And I said, yes.

199
00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:01.410
Phyllis, that is amazing.

200
00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:02.970
That is so crazy.

201
00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:04.310
And you know what?

202
00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:09.370
We know from being on a set, half the time I found out things from my character from the costume department.

203
00:11:09.530 --> 00:11:15.250
And it's just so wild to me that the moment you realized you had a part was because Wardrobe called you.

204
00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:16.370
Because Wardrobe called.

205
00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:17.150
Yeah, exactly.

206
00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:21.450
And the other part of it was I was paid up on my dues.

207
00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:31.030
19 years earlier, I had done one SAG job here in St. Louis, got my SAG card, and I paid my dues every year.

208
00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:34.730
Not because I thought I was ever – I had thought this ship had sailed.

209
00:11:35.030 --> 00:11:38.890
And it was my way of, like, contributing to the actors.

210
00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:45.050
So for 19 years, I paid my dues, and I was all paid up when I got the office job then.

211
00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:46.190
You were ready.

212
00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:48.270
I was ready, yeah.

213
00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:49.590
Oh, my gosh.

214
00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:51.690
So now you do the pilot.

215
00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:59.170
And it was so crazy for us, Phyllis, because we had been reading with you, like you said, all along.

216
00:11:59.170 --> 00:11:59.530
Uh-huh.

217
00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:04.110
And then now we walk on set, and you are part of the caSt.

218
00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:04.690
Yeah.

219
00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:17.450
I'll tell you, it was the strangest hiring in my estimation of having hired people for 19 years, but I didn't say no.

220
00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:26.150
I've told the story before, Phyllis, that you are responsible for me getting my very first speaking job on television,

221
00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:36.610
because you put my tape for Spin City at the top, and you told Allison, I think she should get it, and then I got it.

222
00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:38.470
And you got it, yes.

223
00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:40.710
Yeah, I remember meeting you, too.

224
00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:42.410
You came in.

225
00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:46.190
It was also the one where Freaks and Geeks.

226
00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:50.230
Oh, yeah, I remember that when I came in to audition for Freaks and Geeks.

227
00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:52.230
I didn't get it, but I remember.

228
00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:55.470
Yeah, you came in for that, and I met you there.

229
00:12:55.590 --> 00:12:58.730
She goes, Phyllis, this is Jenna Fisher from St. Louis.

230
00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:12.350
And then, Angela, I remember when you were hired, we had a different casting session because we were creating the role, and I read with you that day as well.

231
00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:19.030
Not only that day, but, Phyllis, the very first time I went in for all the producers, just a room full of people.

232
00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:20.850
They had a camera on a tripod.

233
00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:23.410
You were reading the role of Michael.

234
00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:36.910
Michael, when Michael fake fires Pam to impress Ryan the temp, and I think it is my belief, Phyllis, and I've never really shared this, that the moment Pam calls Michael a jerk,

235
00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:37.810
right?

236
00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:50.670
I hit that word jerk so hard, probably because I'm a little Southern sometimes and feisty, and the room went quiet, and your reaction was kind of wounded for a split second.

237
00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:54.130
And I think that was the birth of Angela and Phyllis.

238
00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:55.090
I really do.

239
00:13:55.350 --> 00:14:02.430
Because then everyone erupted into laughter, and I remember thinking in my head, oh, wait, I don't think they're supposed to laugh right here.

240
00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:05.110
I think this is supposed to be a sad moment.

241
00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:13.370
It's interesting, isn't it, how your head goes through all of these exercises in a split second when you're auditioning, too, you know?

242
00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:13.690
Yeah.

243
00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:13.990
Yeah.

244
00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:14.990
It's crazy.

245
00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:23.030
But I was so happy to see you when they brought me back in for The Accountant, you know, this role of, they didn't even have a name for her yet.

246
00:14:23.030 --> 00:14:26.150
And I was like, oh, oh, that's who I read with last time.

247
00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:26.910
That's Phyllis.

248
00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:30.590
Boy, we have a history, don't we, ladies?

249
00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:31.430
We do.

250
00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:32.110
We really do.

251
00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:40.710
I was just going to tell you, Phyllis, you know, Jenna and I are re-watching the show and seeing some of these episodes for the very first time since they aired, you know?

252
00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:48.390
And we keep talking about characters we're falling back in love with, and Phyllis is hands down one of those characters.

253
00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:51.090
Everything you do on this show is brilliant.

254
00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:58.650
Every single line you say, every look you give, it should be like an actor's study class to watch you on this show.

255
00:14:58.650 --> 00:14:59.610
Well, thank you.

256
00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:02.690
I just went and tried to do it the beSt.

257
00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:03.230
I don't know.

258
00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:03.950
I couldn't.

259
00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:07.350
You know, I always had my sides there hidden underneath my.

260
00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:10.650
In your desk drawer, you would sometimes have them in that top drawer.

261
00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:15.510
Yeah, everybody would come and say, no, my sides were there and use them, too, you know.

262
00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:16.190
I would.

263
00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:21.370
I was always losing mine, but I knew where yours were.

264
00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:26.970
And you were welcome to use them, too.

265
00:15:28.090 --> 00:15:32.970
Phyllis, I have to ask you about your iconic Phyllis look.

266
00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:41.050
As we've been re-watching the show, we have really noticed how characters have a certain wardrobe look.

267
00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:45.990
And Phyllis, the character, was in a lot of purple tones.

268
00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:46.930
Yes.

269
00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:52.050
And with the big necklaces that were almost a character in and of themselves.

270
00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:52.710
Right.

271
00:15:54.350 --> 00:16:01.530
I'm curious, did you have much input into this look when you met with Carrie Bennett for that first time?

272
00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:02.850
How did this look come about?

273
00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:08.430
No, because I was so fresh on the scene as an actor.

274
00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:10.150
I didn't know you could have input.

275
00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:20.290
So I just went, stood there, let him put the clothes on me, made silly faces as they took the pictures.

276
00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:23.250
I didn't want my arms to be flopping around.

277
00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:24.750
I knew that.

278
00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:26.770
So I wanted my arms covered.

279
00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:31.430
I didn't want him to look like I was landing planes in the office.

280
00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:39.550
But that was about the only real parameter that I gave, was that I didn't want my arms to show.

281
00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:46.230
And the jewel tones just kind of came about, I guess, with my coloring and my eye color and stuff.

282
00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:49.230
They just started developing the different necklaces.

283
00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:53.750
They actually made a lot of them to make them go with the outfits.

284
00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:54.990
Did they really?

285
00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:56.410
I didn't know that.

286
00:16:56.410 --> 00:17:03.950
Yeah, some of them, a lot were bought, but some were made or adjusted or two or three put together, you know.

287
00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:06.750
And so for me, it was always interesting.

288
00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:16.690
We'd go in for a wardrobe fitting to see what we were actually going to be to wear, you know, what would be in our trailers to put on.

289
00:17:16.870 --> 00:17:24.750
Because some of the outfits that we started in the beginning were there, but a lot of them had changed just kind of miraculously.

290
00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:26.650
You would have a surprise.

291
00:17:27.010 --> 00:17:27.550
Yes.

292
00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:39.850
Well, it's funny you say that because, you know, on a lot of shows, most projects I've done, you do pick a full look and then that look ends up in your trailer.

293
00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:45.790
But with the office, I felt like we were creating a closet of mix and match clothes.

294
00:17:46.030 --> 00:17:47.610
And so it's true.

295
00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:54.630
I didn't know exactly which shirt and which blouse and which cardigan were going to be put together each week.

296
00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:59.330
Because they made a whole closet for our character, and it was very mix and match.

297
00:17:59.810 --> 00:18:00.230
Exactly.

298
00:18:00.530 --> 00:18:03.830
I loved going into the costume trailer for our fittings.

299
00:18:03.970 --> 00:18:09.930
And if you walked in the back, every character had a whole row, like a whole row of clothes.

300
00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:15.490
So you could walk down the Dwight row of costumes, and it was mostly mustard-colored shirts.

301
00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:19.590
But it was really interesting to see everyone's, like, row of clothes.

302
00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:20.070
Yeah.

303
00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:26.230
And you could actually, like you said, the character stood out by just the look.

304
00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:29.770
Yeah, they wouldn't even have to, yeah, they would not have had to have a label.

305
00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:30.450
You would know.

306
00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:30.770
Right.

307
00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:35.290
You would know where Pam was and Angela and all of us, yeah.

308
00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:37.710
And Meredith, poor Meredith.

309
00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:39.390
Kate.

310
00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:40.470
Kate did.

311
00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:43.090
She had some real special outfits.

312
00:18:43.410 --> 00:18:47.730
Some crazy corduroy jumpers and sweater vests.

313
00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:53.130
Well, you know what, Phyllis, we want to ask you a question that I always love to hear

314
00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:58.030
everyone's answers, is that did you have a favorite episode or scene from your time on

315
00:18:58.030 --> 00:19:00.410
the show, or maybe a Phyllis storyline?

316
00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:02.610
All the above.

317
00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:11.790
As far as favorite episodes, there's so many that it's hard to, like, just choose one.

318
00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:12.510
I agree.

319
00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:16.630
But, and a lot of bits and pieces from various episodes.

320
00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:24.150
Like, I love the scene when Jim in Casino Night tells Pam that he's in love with her out

321
00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:25.070
on the parking lot.

322
00:19:25.550 --> 00:19:30.670
That scene always, still to this day, it makes me get a lump in my throat.

323
00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:36.210
And then when he comes in and sweeps her off her feet, that's even more miraculous, you

324
00:19:36.210 --> 00:19:36.450
know?

325
00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:41.110
So, and I love that scene.

326
00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:45.850
And then there's this silly scene between Dwight and Michael where they're standing out in

327
00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:53.950
front of Andy Buckley's character's house, and they thought that they had, were the ones

328
00:19:53.950 --> 00:19:55.990
that kept the office from closing.

329
00:19:56.230 --> 00:19:56.750
Yes.

330
00:19:56.890 --> 00:19:57.290
Yes.

331
00:19:57.290 --> 00:20:01.850
And they high-five, and then one says to the other, I don't know.

332
00:20:01.910 --> 00:20:02.590
How did we do it?

333
00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:03.130
I don't know.

334
00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:07.070
And they're just like, I don't know what we did, but we did it, you know?

335
00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:11.710
And it's such a stupid little scene, and for some reason, it always stuck out in my mind,

336
00:20:11.710 --> 00:20:12.690
too, you know?

337
00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:15.470
And then there's, close your mouth, sweetie.

338
00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:16.630
You look like a trout.

339
00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:19.630
That's what Jenna and I.

340
00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:25.510
And then Angela, we had our whole Moroccan Christmas where we just, when you put that

341
00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:32.990
hairnet on, and I mean, you put your hairnet on, that whole episode was pretty special.

342
00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:40.150
Phyllis, my kids lost their minds when they watched the scene between you and I where I

343
00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:43.370
come up to you, and I'm like, Phyllis, these are forks.

344
00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:47.390
That scene, they couldn't get over it.

345
00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:51.170
Yes, when you're explaining everything.

346
00:20:51.890 --> 00:20:55.310
They'll say to me in the kitchen sometimes, Mom, this is a fork.

347
00:20:55.550 --> 00:20:57.030
I'm like, okay, ha-ha.

348
00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:02.190
Yeah, we had some really great scenes, didn't we?

349
00:21:02.330 --> 00:21:02.570
We did.

350
00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:11.670
One of my favorite storylines, Phyllis, is when we find out that you do not like the

351
00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:15.250
office administrator over at Vance Refrigeration.

352
00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:15.790
Yes.

353
00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:16.770
Oh, yeah.

354
00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:18.710
You do not care for her.

355
00:21:19.330 --> 00:21:22.930
Because she looks pretty much like me, you know, first off.

356
00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:24.110
Younger, though.

357
00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:25.170
Yes.

358
00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:33.130
And Pam sees this woman as kind of a mentor, and that is a storyline I wanted to see more

359
00:21:33.130 --> 00:21:33.370
of.

360
00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:38.130
I really, really, I could have watched that for multiple episodes.

361
00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:44.690
I guess maybe I put a wise word into Bob or something, because she didn't come back.

362
00:21:44.970 --> 00:21:45.830
She did not.

363
00:21:46.830 --> 00:21:47.570
That's right.

364
00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:54.010
I tell you, the one scene I had a really hard time not laughing through was when Steve and

365
00:21:54.010 --> 00:22:00.330
I at my wedding, when he came into the bride's room to give me advice.

366
00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:09.390
And we could not get through the word pungent because I had let it toot.

367
00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:15.490
And every time he would say pungent, he would break out in his cackle.

368
00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:19.530
He couldn't, he couldn't get through it, and I couldn't get through it.

369
00:22:19.630 --> 00:22:24.110
And then we hear the sound people sitting outside the door laughing.

370
00:22:24.110 --> 00:22:30.670
So they had to move them down the stairway, down into the bottom, because every time we

371
00:22:30.670 --> 00:22:33.010
said pungent, we lost it.

372
00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:35.690
I finally, the only way I got through was to hold my breath.

373
00:22:35.990 --> 00:22:40.030
When he said pungent, I just held my breath until the end of the scene.

374
00:22:40.210 --> 00:22:43.690
So if I look a little blue in that scene, that's the reason.

375
00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:47.530
I also noticed you look down, like at your feet.

376
00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:51.510
Like you were like, I cannot make eye contact with this man right now.

377
00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:05.600
Phyllis, is there a moment from our time on The Office?

378
00:23:05.600 --> 00:23:09.540
It could be anything, you know, on set or off set.

379
00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:15.020
If you could go back and relive a moment, if you could go sit in that moment again,

380
00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:16.920
what would you like to do?

381
00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:21.620
I would like to redo when we won the Emmy.

382
00:23:21.920 --> 00:23:26.680
When we all ran up on stage, we were told we shouldn't go.

383
00:23:26.820 --> 00:23:28.120
We had to stay in the audience.

384
00:23:28.120 --> 00:23:29.700
We weren't allowed to go up.

385
00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:32.160
It was a long shot that we were going to win.

386
00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:35.920
And when we did win, we all just, you know, ran to the stage.

387
00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:41.880
And I love the joy that everybody had, you know, because that was our Emmy, you know,

388
00:23:42.020 --> 00:23:42.740
that we won.

389
00:23:43.060 --> 00:23:46.060
So I love that moment for that reason.

390
00:23:46.260 --> 00:23:49.900
And then, Angela, were you in the boat with me when we went back?

391
00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:50.980
Yes, Phyllis.

392
00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:51.380
Yes.

393
00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:57.180
But only people only remember that it was you on the boat.

394
00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:00.440
Like, Jenna's memory was like, remember when Phyllis got lost at sea?

395
00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:05.220
And I was like, hello, I was on the boat, too, with Rain and Brian.

396
00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:09.460
You said, Brian, you, me, the wardrobe lady.

397
00:24:09.900 --> 00:24:10.780
And Rain.

398
00:24:10.780 --> 00:24:12.300
Rain and rain.

399
00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:15.960
And that was a very memorable night.

400
00:24:16.400 --> 00:24:19.580
We were stranded out there in the middle of the ocean.

401
00:24:19.820 --> 00:24:22.680
Pitch darkness, just drifting out to sea.

402
00:24:24.520 --> 00:24:27.360
And then they wanted us to transfer boats.

403
00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:28.560
Oh, Phyllis.

404
00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:33.500
To stand up and actually go to the other boat was frightening because they were holding it

405
00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:34.220
with their foot.

406
00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:35.120
Yes.

407
00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:36.180
This is the thing.

408
00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:41.660
I tried to explain this in our book that Jenna and I wrote, and I really don't think I did

409
00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:46.960
it justice because these two rubber dinghies were being held together by just one man,

410
00:24:46.960 --> 00:24:48.880
like, straddling them, you know?

411
00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:49.180
Yeah.

412
00:24:49.380 --> 00:24:51.140
We're trying to climb over.

413
00:24:51.460 --> 00:24:51.940
Yeah.

414
00:24:52.460 --> 00:24:53.740
And we're going, what?

415
00:24:54.020 --> 00:24:54.460
Yeah.

416
00:24:54.460 --> 00:24:56.160
And it's bobbing up and down.

417
00:24:56.340 --> 00:24:57.760
And I'm like, how are we doing this?

418
00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:01.240
Once we got back to shore, it seemed okay.

419
00:25:01.240 --> 00:25:03.660
But in the middle of it, it was pretty frightening.

420
00:25:04.400 --> 00:25:04.660
Yeah.

421
00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:09.980
We also love to ask everyone what they took from set when we wrapped, because Jenna and

422
00:25:09.980 --> 00:25:11.020
I took a lot of things.

423
00:25:11.500 --> 00:25:12.180
Let's see.

424
00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:18.160
I took the bird that Stanley carved for me when he was down in Florida.

425
00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:19.000
I have that.

426
00:25:19.240 --> 00:25:27.240
I wanted my Bushiest Beaver Award, but it seemed to have gotten lost somewhere along the side.

427
00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:30.800
But Phyllis, you need the Bushiest Beaver Award in your home.

428
00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:33.080
On the wall.

429
00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:34.360
Yeah.

430
00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:35.260
I need that.

431
00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:39.880
And I would have liked to have had the oven mitt, but I don't remember getting that either

432
00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:41.280
that I made for Michael.

433
00:25:41.600 --> 00:25:42.200
Oh, yeah.

434
00:25:42.560 --> 00:25:44.080
That scene was hilarious.

435
00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:50.700
Talk about brilliant performance by you when Michael hates the oven mitt so much.

436
00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:52.900
Oh, Phyllis.

437
00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:53.760
Yeah.

438
00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:56.440
We had some great times, didn't we, ladies?

439
00:25:56.740 --> 00:25:57.440
We did.

440
00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:58.880
We really did.

441
00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:04.040
It's been such a joy to go back and rewatch because it just opens the floodgates of memories.

442
00:26:04.180 --> 00:26:08.320
So we did a lot of weird, funny, good, odd things in our nine years.

443
00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:09.560
We did.

444
00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:15.280
You know, Phyllis, one of the things Jen and I love is that we get the call sheets from

445
00:26:15.280 --> 00:26:20.080
Steve Burgess, and on the back of them are these cast and crew questions where they would

446
00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:23.580
pick a member of the cast and crew and ask them five questions.

447
00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:27.820
And so we would love to ask you the call sheet questions.

448
00:26:28.160 --> 00:26:30.820
I just have delighted in them.

449
00:26:31.060 --> 00:26:31.960
Are you ready?

450
00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:33.100
Okay.

451
00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:34.720
All right.

452
00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:36.100
Here's a good one.

453
00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:39.880
Number one, what was your first entertainment job?

454
00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:43.960
Oh, my first entertainment job was dancing.

455
00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:51.600
I got paid a dollar at the Carpenters Hall here in St. Louis on Hampton Avenue, and I did

456
00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:56.240
a toe dance to a doll coming out of a doll box.

457
00:26:56.460 --> 00:26:57.980
I got paid a dollar.

458
00:26:58.440 --> 00:26:59.680
How old were you?

459
00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:04.280
I was at a point, so I was about eight years old.

460
00:27:05.100 --> 00:27:05.700
Wow.

461
00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:08.620
That was my first job.

462
00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:10.320
And I still have the dollar.

463
00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:11.880
Do you really?

464
00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:12.880
I do.

465
00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:18.200
I folded it up and put it in the picture of that particular dance.

466
00:27:18.200 --> 00:27:18.580
Yeah.

467
00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:20.520
I would love to see that.

468
00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:24.800
You were dancing on point at eight years old, Phyllis?

469
00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:29.040
Yeah, I started dancing at seven, and she put me on point.

470
00:27:29.220 --> 00:27:34.500
I was just about eight, probably going into nine, you know, like closer to nine.

471
00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:37.940
But I was about eight years old when she put me on point.

472
00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:38.300
Yeah.

473
00:27:38.580 --> 00:27:42.560
I mean, this is something that I think our listeners know about you, but we should remind

474
00:27:42.560 --> 00:27:47.580
you that you are a phenomenal dancer, that you danced for a long time.

475
00:27:48.050 --> 00:27:48.600
I did.

476
00:27:48.820 --> 00:27:55.740
I danced until I was in my, I would say, 32 or so professionally.

477
00:27:56.220 --> 00:28:02.320
And then it got to the point it was, you know, hard to compete with the 18-year-olds.

478
00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:04.860
But yeah, I danced.

479
00:28:04.900 --> 00:28:05.800
I did it all.

480
00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:06.680
I did ballet.

481
00:28:07.540 --> 00:28:09.400
I was in two ballet companies.

482
00:28:09.400 --> 00:28:13.740
I was in a jazz company in San Diego, the jazz company.

483
00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:18.460
I toured it when dinner theater was around.

484
00:28:18.740 --> 00:28:23.860
I was in a vaudeville burlesque show for, we toured about six, seven years.

485
00:28:24.340 --> 00:28:31.880
And then I was in another show in Long Beach, California, where we performed three shows Saturday,

486
00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:32.960
three shows Sunday.

487
00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:34.000
Wow.

488
00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:37.360
But we got paid enough to, for the whole week.

489
00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:39.400
So it was pretty lucrative, you know.

490
00:28:39.640 --> 00:28:40.500
It was okay.

491
00:28:40.500 --> 00:28:43.040
So yeah, my career started out as a dancer.

492
00:28:43.620 --> 00:28:47.620
Then when I got too old and injured, then I ended up going into casting.

